WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.430
A family has to sacrifice one of their own
to save the world.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:05.430
Can they do it?

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.019
Here's what happens after three people hear
a Knock at the Cabin.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.900
Knock at the Cabin centers around a choice
that's posed to couple Andrew and Eric, and

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:14.934
their young daughter Wen.

00:00:14.934 --> 00:00:19.460
Four strangers arrive at the cabin where Andrew,
Eric, and Wen are staying and take control

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:20.460
of the house.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.359
They tie up Andrew and Eric and tell them
that the four of them have been united by

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:29.740
a shared vision of an apocalyptic event that
will end humanity if a choice isn't made.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.760
Andrew, Eric, and Wen are tasked with picking
which one of them will be sacrificed to stop

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.580
the world from ending.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:42.059
Every time the family says no to the sacrifice,
one of the four strangers dies, and a deadly

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:44.440
plague gets unleashed on the world.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:49.360
If it gets to the point where all four of
the strangers die, then Andrew, Eric, and

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.940
Wen will live only long enough to watch the
rest of the world burn around them.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.680
It's a dilemma that at one point is described
as an impossible choice, and it leads to bloodshed

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.440
as Andrew and Eric each wrestle with believing
the strangers and finding a way out.

00:01:03.662 --> 00:01:08.750
The central choice in Knock at the Cabin is
an example of a common ethics thought experiment

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.360
called "the trolley problem."

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.500
This scenario imagines someone being told
that a trolley's brakes have failed and it

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.880
is barreling towards a group of people tied
to the tracks.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.000
This bystander has the chance to intervene
and flip a switch that would send the trolley

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.510
on an adjacent track, where it would kill
just one person.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.439
In Knock at the Cabin, Andrew, Eric, and Wen
deal with this dilemma.

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:34.703
It's fitting that Leonard, the leader of the
four strangers, defines this as an "impossible choice."

00:01:34.703 --> 00:01:37.087
After all, it's not just about numbers.

00:01:37.087 --> 00:01:41.210
These three characters are forced to measure
the weight of their own lives against the

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.290
lives of anonymous strangers.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.190
Knock at the Cabin elevates the trolley problem
to a grander and more emotional scale, which

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.549
is partly why the film is so engaging and
relatable.

00:01:51.771 --> 00:01:57.420
As Eric and Andrew refuse to make the choice
and the strangers' deaths unleash horrifying

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.350
plagues on humanity, viewers get to see Andrew
and Eric's life together before this encounter.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:07.570
A series of flashbacks shows Eric and Andrew
going to adopt Wen, Andrew being attacked

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:12.010
at a bar by a homophobic patron, and the three
of them arriving at the cabin.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.459
So, what's the importance of these flashbacks?

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:19.226
Ultimately, they embody the reason that Eric
and Andrew simply can't sacrifice one of their own.

00:02:19.226 --> 00:02:23.780
From dealing with disapproval from their parents
and the outside world to becoming parents

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.020
themselves, these two have been through a
lot together.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:31.930
There's a deep love between Andrew and Eric
that's hard to break, which Leonard even acknowledges.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:36.040
"I believe you were chosen because your family's
love for each other is so pure."

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.010
These flashbacks play a pivotal role in helping
viewers understand Eric and Andrew's inability

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.562
to make this choice, even as they are told
the consequences.

00:02:44.813 --> 00:02:48.159
Throughout Knock at the Cabin, Andrew is the
voice of reason.

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:52.459
He refuses to believe what the strangers are
saying, and his strong-willed logic leaves

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:54.540
viewers unsure of what's real.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.519
For example, Andrew points out that the news
broadcasts the strangers show them, which

00:02:58.519 --> 00:03:02.700
depict apocalyptic events, could have simply
been carefully timed.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.379
Perhaps these news stories were already in
the works, and this could just be a matter

00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:10.650
of the strangers knowing what was going to
happen beforehand and using that information

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.010
to scare the family.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:17.005
More significantly, Andrew believes that they
have been targeted because he and Eric are gay.

00:03:17.005 --> 00:03:20.510
He thinks that Redmond might be the man who
attacked him at the bar.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:24.709
His conviction is so strong that he gets Sabrina
and Adriane to doubt their own beliefs.

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:28.880
However, as it becomes clearer that there
might be more at play than Eric and Andrew

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.510
initially thought, Andrew's logical thinking
reveals itself to be a cover for the fear

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.790
he's feeling about the inevitable.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.180
Andrew is arguably the most complex and compelling
character due to the way he makes viewers

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.098
question their own beliefs.

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:43.234
"It doesn't matter.

00:03:43.234 --> 00:03:44.215
None of us believe you."

00:03:44.417 --> 00:03:48.560
During the first ritual sequence where the
strangers kill Redmond in order to release

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.019
a plague, Eric notices something in the light
shining through the window.

00:03:52.019 --> 00:03:56.150
In a quick flash, the light-filled visage
of a figure can be seen.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:00.459
Throughout the film, Eric questions what he
saw and if it was real or just a byproduct

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:01.730
of his concussion.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:05.870
As the truth is unveiled, it becomes clear
that this figure in the light wasn't just

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.489
a figment of Eric's imagination.

00:04:07.489 --> 00:04:12.810
It's never explicitly said who or what Eric
saw, but based on the themes of the film,

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:15.040
we can consider different interpretations
of it.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.919
Knock at the Cabin centers around the question
of belief and what happens when our beliefs

00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:21.799
of the world and its rules get challenged.

00:04:21.799 --> 00:04:26.350
Andrew's worldview holds no room for even
the possibility that what the strangers are

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:27.990
saying is true.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:33.350
Eric is less sure of himself, so perhaps this
figure of light is a mystical being, or even

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:37.630
God coming down to Earth to try to convince
him that there's something more happening.

00:04:37.774 --> 00:04:43.520
Now, for the million-dollar question: Will
the world really end if Eric and Andrew refuse

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.240
to sacrifice one of their own?

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.800
The answer, shockingly, is yes.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.080
While it initially seems like maybe Andrew
was right and that the strangers' visions

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.960
were just delusions, it becomes clear that
they weren't lying once Leonard is the only

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:57.960
one left.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:02.910
When Leonard is the last stranger left, he
shows Eric and Andrew a newscast of hundreds

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:05.080
of planes crashing out of the sky.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.510
At this point, both the characters and viewers
realize that it's a bit too far-fetched to

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.910
believe that the strangers coordinated something
on that scale.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:17.479
It's enough to make anyone believe that this
isn't a hoax, after all.

00:05:17.479 --> 00:05:22.600
Eric and Andrew are stunned by this revelation
and are left with one more choice to try and

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.400
stop the world from ending.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:30.240
Dave Bautista's incredible performance as
Leonard sells this shocking twist perfectly.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.190
In this moment, the weight of every disaster
that's happened throughout the film crashes

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.190
down on you.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:41.069
M. Night Shyamalan doesn't follow this up
with some other twist that disproves this

00:05:41.069 --> 00:05:46.530
sudden realization, so it's really true: The
things the strangers said would happen are

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:51.259
happening, and because Eric and Andrew struggled
to believe them, the world is now on the verge

00:05:51.259 --> 00:05:52.259
of ending.

00:05:52.259 --> 00:05:54.232
"Will you make a choice?"

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:59.710
When Leonard finds himself as the only stranger
remaining, he decides to have one last exchange

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.110
with Eric and Andrew outside the cabin.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:07.020
He further divulges his motivations for coming
to them and what he saw in the visions.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.669
Leonard describes the visions of the apocalypse
as nightmarish and says he felt completely

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:14.060
helpless seeing the world burn.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:18.470
That sense of dread and the heart-wrenching
idea that the ones that Leonard loves would

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:23.210
perish in a fiery apocalypse drove him to
lead this small group of seers.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:28.370
Now, it's all he thinks about, since the world
is on the brink of destruction.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:33.539
After spilling his guts this last time to
Eric and Andrew, he asks them if they'll make

00:06:33.539 --> 00:06:34.539
the choice.

00:06:34.539 --> 00:06:39.289
Although the audience isn't shown their response,
Leonard's actions say it all: Leonard draws

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:44.020
a knife and says that humanity has been judged
before taking his own life.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:48.430
It's a subtle but powerful shift as up until
now, Leonard and the other strangers have

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:51.990
talked about the world facing judgment, which
could go either way.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:57.060
Here, Leonard, as the last stranger standing,
makes a definitive statement that seems to

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.010
seal humanity's fate.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:04.430
With his death, Eric and Andrew have mere
minutes to make a choice to try to stop the end.

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:09.639
At first glance, the four strangers that arrive
at the cabin seem like four ordinary people.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:13.860
When they first introduce themselves to Eric,
Andrew, and Wen, they talk about their lives.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:19.069
So, it's tough not to wonder why they were
chosen and if they serve a greater purpose.

00:07:19.069 --> 00:07:24.110
Shortly after Leonard dies and Eric gains
immense clarity, he comes to the conclusion

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:25.350
that they do.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:29.389
Eric believes that based on everything that's
happened and the distinct qualities that each

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:34.210
of the strangers has, these four people embody
the Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse.

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:38.039
In the Book of Revelations, the Four Horsemen
of the Apocalypse are seen as the harbingers

00:07:38.039 --> 00:07:43.720
of the end of time, and each symbolizes a
different end of humanity: conquest, war,

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:45.069
famine, and death.

00:07:45.069 --> 00:07:50.099
Here though, Eric interprets each stranger
as a positive quality of humanity, rather

00:07:50.099 --> 00:07:52.129
than as a means to its end.

00:07:52.129 --> 00:07:56.979
For instance, Leonard personifies guidance,
as seen in the way he leads the group and

00:07:58.599 --> 00:08:01.110
"It doesn't have to be like this."

00:08:01.266 --> 00:08:06.075
But now that the four of them have been snuffed
out, humanity's best qualities are gone with

00:08:06.075 --> 00:08:09.005
them, leaving the world open to destruction.

00:08:09.005 --> 00:08:14.116
It's a crushing realization overall that not
only gives deeper meaning to the four strangers,

00:08:14.116 --> 00:08:17.295
but also makes Eric realize that a choice
needs to be made.

00:08:17.828 --> 00:08:23.915
With only minutes left until the world's fate
is sealed, Eric offers himself up to be sacrificed.

00:08:23.915 --> 00:08:28.005
Over the course of the film, Eric has become
the most connected with the strangers and

00:08:28.005 --> 00:08:30.956
has been less resistant to their mission than
Andrew.

00:08:30.956 --> 00:08:34.575
That — plus his experience of seeing the
figure in the light — has opened him up

00:08:34.575 --> 00:08:38.115
to the truth of what's happening, even if
he wishes it weren't so.

00:08:38.115 --> 00:08:42.860
He comes to see that the only way for Wen
to live a full life is for him to make the choice.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.446
"There was something in the light."

00:08:44.446 --> 00:08:49.756
Eric is in a clearer state of mind than Andrew
and has found a euphoric peace in thinking

00:08:49.756 --> 00:08:52.745
about Andrew and Wen and their future together.

00:08:52.745 --> 00:08:56.946
Eric delivers a monologue that pairs with
a flash forward, which shows viewers exactly

00:08:56.946 --> 00:09:01.216
what he hopes for: Andrew and Wen, both older,
laughing together.

00:09:01.216 --> 00:09:05.256
It's the last memory he wants to think about
before his death.

00:09:05.256 --> 00:09:09.745
Eric is ready to make the sacrifice that will
give the ones he loves a chance at a real

00:09:09.745 --> 00:09:11.985
future in an imperfect world.

00:09:11.985 --> 00:09:17.146
Although Andrew struggles at first, he eventually
finds the strength to shoot Eric in the chest.

00:09:17.146 --> 00:09:20.327
Thus, with Eric's sacrifice, the choice is
made.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:25.526
So, with Andrew and Eric making their choice
and Eric dying to save his family, the only

00:09:25.526 --> 00:09:27.946
question that remains is: Was it in time?

00:09:27.946 --> 00:09:32.486
At first, it doesn't seem like it since the
skies are still dark and stormy.

00:09:32.486 --> 00:09:37.006
Lightning strikes all around Andrew as he
climbs into the treehouse to see Wen.

00:09:37.006 --> 00:09:41.226
It feels like the world is collapsing around
them and the two of them weep over the loss

00:09:41.226 --> 00:09:42.226
of Eric.

00:09:42.226 --> 00:09:46.446
The two of them head to a local diner, where
they discover that Eric's sacrifice came just

00:09:46.446 --> 00:09:48.206
in time to save the world.

00:09:48.206 --> 00:09:52.896
At the diner, Andrew and Wen see news reports
about the disasters stopping: The water from

00:09:52.896 --> 00:09:57.836
the tsunamis starts to recede, those affected
by viral outbreak begin to recover, and planes

00:09:57.836 --> 00:09:59.956
stop falling from the skies.

00:09:59.956 --> 00:10:03.946
Even the rain that now falls around them is
putting out fires from the plane crashes.

00:10:03.946 --> 00:10:06.556
The world starts to feel hopeful again.

00:10:06.556 --> 00:10:11.016
After such an intense turn of events in the
film, this reveal gives viewers a meaningful

00:10:11.016 --> 00:10:15.476
moment of tranquility and shows that Eric's
sacrifice had real meaning.

